WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:04.220
Film Courage: When Millie approached you after
the course in Riverside and said “I’m

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:05.580
so silly, I did this.”

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.001
You saw something in her that said well if
the other writers said “I don’t want to

00:00:09.001 --> 00:00:14.660
take THE GRAPES OF WRATH and outline it well
then they believe in this romance of being

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:17.949
able to write and I’m just going to sort
of sit down and take a bottle of Scotch and

00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:20.460
a cigarette and pound away all of the things
that happened to me.”

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.539
Dr. Ken Atchity, author/producer: Right, they’re
in love with image of being a writer.

00:00:22.539 --> 00:00:27.190
You know it’s the romance of being a writer,
the struggle, the torment, the agony and the

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:36.070
ecstasy, all of those grandiose concepts that
writers have done to get bigger paydays, you

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:37.070
know that’s all good.

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:42.180
Like I had a writer (who was been a client
for years) a dear friend who we sold like

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:48.609
12 of his screenplays and it took him (honestly)
a week to write a screenplay.

00:00:48.609 --> 00:00:54.640
And it took me three years to train him never,
never to admit that.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:59.550
And so I got him an agent and the agent was
always beating him up and saying “You can’t

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.199
say that.

00:01:01.199 --> 00:01:03.399
You have to say you need 3 months.

00:01:03.399 --> 00:01:05.040
6 months or whatever.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.890
You can’t say you do it in a week.”

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:10.510
They won’t…we’re trying to get a million
dollars for this.”

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:14.520
And the truth is you can do it in a week.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:21.240
But you can put it aside for two months and
go back and work on it some more and so on.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:28.840
Because the reality of writing is much easier
than the myth of it.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:36.560
Walker Percy one of my favorite novelist who
wrote The Last Picture Show said “Perhaps

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.170
the secret of speaking is having something
to say."

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:44.390
And that’s what it’s all about, you have
story to tell, then you can tell it, it’s

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:45.390
not a problem.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:49.350
Just don’t start writing it until you know
the story.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:53.220
Like you don’t start telling the joke before
you know the joke.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:59.300
You don’t walk in with 3 by 5 cards and
tell a joke from the 3 by 5 card.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.980
You know the thing by heart and then you tell
it.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:08.050
It’s not the agony, it doesn’t need to
be the agony and we it doesn’t have the

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:14.250
structure because when you haven’t learned
the mechanics of it like two kinds of productive

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:15.260
writers.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:16.970
When you learn the mechanics then you can
be happy.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:18.810
And you can do it whenever you want to do
it.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:23.970
Like any writer who knows it will tell you
they can write on trains, busses, planes,

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.200
they can write standing up, sitting down.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:32.200
I had a dear friend my client for years Nancy
Friedman who wrote on her back for the last

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:37.090
10 years of her life because she couldn’t
sit up and she couldn’t stand up and so

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:40.060
she wrote on her back and she just kept writing
books.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:44.290
And the writing is easier if you know what
you’re doing.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:50.260
And whether it’s Harlan Ellison or Ray Bradbury,
they’ll all tell you the same thing.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:55.350
But when you think it’s this huge agony
and it is (like Virginia Woolf and so on),

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.400
she’s never figured it out, that’s why.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.209
She’s believed that it has to be an agony
to be good.

00:03:03.209 --> 00:03:05.310
And I don’t think Shakespeare was in agony.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:10.150
I think he was just dashing this stuff off
as fast as he could write because he was reading

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:16.070
everything that was coming from Italy and
you know South of Europe and stealing it like

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:22.880
crazy, redoing it, copying it, he heard stories
all around and he just couldn’t wait before

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:27.349
going to the pub and after going to the pub
to sit down and dash it off.

00:03:27.349 --> 00:03:31.750
And he revised, he spent a lot of time revising.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:38.569
We know that from the folios but it’s not
the living in the garret starving thing that

00:03:38.569 --> 00:03:40.209
has to happen at all.

00:03:40.209 --> 00:03:42.320
It’s just figuring out what you’re doing.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.340
It’s a craft, you know, it’s a craft that
has to be learned.

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:49.880
And the art comes in, there are brilliant
people who do the craft and there are not

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:53.260
so brilliant people who do the craft.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:59.319
So there is plenty of room for art but it
doesn’t have to be cutting your ear off

00:03:59.319 --> 00:03:59.950
kind of thing.

